# Example Bank — code owners.
#
# This file is here because the bank already has one. pen.dev does not ship an
# approvals model: an exception routes to whoever owns the thing in CODEOWNERS,
# which is already argued over, already maintained, and already enforced on the
# same merge button the required check blocks.
#
# The join is the slug. `group:cards-platform` in catalog/catalog-info.yaml finds
# @examplebank/cards-platform here. When no handle matches, the check says "no
# CODEOWNERS entry" rather than inventing a plausible one — a refusal routed to the
# wrong team is worse than one routed nowhere.

# The service catalog is the rule everything else is judged against. Changing it
# changes what may ship, so the platform group reviews every edit to it.
/catalog/                       @examplebank/platform-architecture

# Each API specification is owned by the group that owns the service.
/apis/cards-*.openapi.yaml      @examplebank/cards-platform
/apis/accounts-*.openapi.yaml   @examplebank/accounts-platform
/apis/identity-*.openapi.yaml   @examplebank/identity-platform

# The approved component library and the design that consumes it.
/components/                    @examplebank/channel-design-system
/design/                        @examplebank/retail-channel

# What each element on a screen stands on. This is the file the required check
# annotates line by line when it refuses.
/enterprise/                    @examplebank/retail-channel @examplebank/platform-architecture

# The runtime. Client extensions are deployed by the channel platform team.
/liferay-workspace/             @examplebank/channel-platform

# Proposed seams land here before anyone builds them. Two groups on purpose: the
# team who needs it, and the architects who decide whether it should exist.
/seams/                         @examplebank/platform-architecture @examplebank/retail-channel

# EXCEPTIONS TO THE EXPORT GATE. Committing a file here is the request, and this
# line is what turns it into an approval: the path routes the review to a group,
# and branch protection will not let the pull request merge without them.
#
# ORDER MATTERS. GitHub takes the LAST matching pattern, not the most specific
# one — so this catch-all is the floor, and a per-waiver line that routes an
# exception to the team who owns that capability must come BELOW it.
/.pen/waivers/**                @examplebank/platform-architecture

# Routed to the team who owns this capability. BELOW the catch-all above,
# because GitHub takes the last matching pattern.
/.pen/waivers/dashboard-cards-tile.yaml  @examplebank/cards-platform
